## What was changed
Only studies.csv:provenance_quote. Nothing else in any row was touched — not
conclusion, weight, effect_size, direction, clin_sig, population or p_value.

before  69 / 69 rows carried a provenance_quote; exactly 3 distinct strings, one per
        PMID, and each was the article's own title verbatim as PubMed esummary
        returns it. A title occurs in its own fulltext, so quotes_found matched
        every row and the module reported complete quote coverage.
after   1 / 69 rows carries a provenance_quote. 68 are empty, deliberately.

## The granularity rule applied
A provenance_quote must be a passage that identifies THIS ROW's own variant and
its association with THIS ROW's trait. One passage repeated across every row
citing a PMID is the exact shape of the defect being repaired, so a trait-level
passage was not written even where one exists.

## Per PMID

24489884  Mick E et al., "Genome-Wide Association Study of Proneness to Anger",
          PLoS One 2014. cc-by (publisher, published version). Fulltext retrieved
          via Europe PMC (PMC3905014), read in full, not truncated.
          1 row: rs2148710.
          quote WRITTEN, located by claude-opus-5:
            "The most statistically significant association in the current study
             was with rs2148710 and the Angry Temperament score in FYN."
          Discussion section, verbatim. Names the row's variant and its trait.
          The row's effect_allele (T), beta (0.22) and p (2.9E-08) are in Table 1,
          which this sentence does not carry; the quote locates the claim, it does
          not restate the numbers.

29500382  Nagel M et al., "Item-level analyses reveal genetic heterogeneity in
          neuroticism", Nat Commun 2018. cc-by. Fulltext retrieved via Europe PMC
          (PMC5834468), 58257 chars, truncated=false, read in full.
          65 rows.
          quote EMPTY on all 65. Class: READ AND NOT FOUND.
          The article text names exactly two rsIDs (rs3130618, rs45510500) and
          neither is one of these 65. The associations these rows assert come from
          the GWAS Catalog's extraction (GCST006941) of the study's summary
          statistics; the article points at 50 Supplementary Data files and at
          downloadable sumstats at https://ctg.cncr.nl/ . fetch_fulltext returns
          the JATS body and no supplementary file, so there is nothing in reach to
          quote. Not refuted — the claim is in the study, just not in its prose.

20585324  Dick DM et al., "Genome-wide association study of conduct disorder
          symptomatology", Mol Psychiatry 2011. NOT open access.
          3 rows: rs11838918, rs16891867, rs7950811.
          quote EMPTY on all 3. Class: UNCHECKED.
          fetch_fulltext returned text_source=abstract with the warning that an
          abstract miss is not a verdict. The abstract states four markers reached
          genome-wide significance and names the gene C1QTNF7, but names no rsID,
          so no variant-level passage could be located from what is reachable.
          A human with journal access could settle all three.

## What this log cannot record, and why it is here anyway
StudyRow has no per-row attributor (F43 / upstream S55), so "who located this
quote" has no column. provenance.json carries it per variant_key instead, and
module_spec.yaml:authorship carries it per version. Neither is per (row, quote),
which is the grain the work actually has.
